SU(2) finite square-boundary insertion exact audit

COMMAND
make -f Makefile.research probe_su2_current_cone_invariance
./probe_su2_current_cone_invariance 6 3

OUTPUT
SU2_CURRENT_CONE_INVARIANCE maximum_level=12 maximum_coefficient=3 profiles=21840 cone_profiles=4081 transforms=22782 failures=3476 log_concave_cone_profiles=252 log_concave_transforms=1131 log_concave_failures=49 square_log_concave_boundary_profiles=10368 square_log_concave_boundary_transforms=58069 square_log_concave_boundary_failures=0 square_log_concave_full_failures=0 diagonal_admissible_profiles=284 diagonal_admissible_open_checks=2463 diagonal_admissible_open_failures=6 boundary_profiles=5545 boundary_transforms=31141 boundary_failures=4924 cone_to_boundary_transforms=22782 cone_to_boundary_failures=2268 log_concave_cone_to_boundary_transforms=1131 log_concave_cone_to_boundary_failures=0 zero_anchor_interval_transforms=13350 zero_anchor_interval_failures=8836 first_failure=(4,2,2,2,-16) first_input=[2,3,0] first_output=[5,11,5] first_log_concave_failure=(4,2,2,2,-16) first_log_concave_input=[2,3,0] first_log_concave_output=[5,11,5] first_square_failure=(-1,-1,-1,-1,0) first_square_input=[] first_square_output=[] first_diagonal_failure=(6,2,4,-1) first_diagonal_input=[1,2,3,0] first_boundary_failure=(6,2,2,-2) first_boundary_input=[1,3,0,0] first_boundary_output=[4,10,7,3] first_cone_to_boundary_failure=(8,2,6,-1) first_cone_to_boundary_input=[1,0,0,1,0] first_cone_to_boundary_output=[1,2,3,3,1] first_log_concave_cone_to_boundary_failure=(-1,-1,-1,0) first_log_concave_cone_to_boundary_input=[] first_log_concave_cone_to_boundary_output=[] first_zero_anchor_interval_failure=(6,2,4,-1) first_zero_anchor_interval_input=[0,1,0,0] first_zero_anchor_interval_output=[1,3,2,1] result=NO_SQUARE_BOUNDARY_OR_FULL_FAILURE

METHOD
For every half-level 1<=K<=6, the audit exhausts every profile whose
K+1 coefficients lie in {0,1,2,3}.  It first tests the abstract
all-radius anchored-current cone and its log-concave subcone under
d -> N_Q^2 d for every 1<=Q<=K.  The displayed input [2,3,0] proves
that even the abstract log-concave current cone is not invariant.
It separately tests the weaker abstract simple-current boundary cone;
the displayed input [1,3,0,0] proves that this cone is not invariant
either.
It also tests whether the complete anchored cone transports to the
simple-current boundary.  The disconnected-support input [1,0,0,1,0]
shows this is false in general, while no failure occurs after imposing
log concavity.
Finally, it checks whether a zero-anchored nonnegative vector with
connected support is sufficient for the reverse residual boundary
inequality.  The displayed input [0,1,0,0] proves that it is not.

Independently, the audit constructs every finite character square
d=p*p.  When d is log-concave and satisfies all simple-current boundary
inequalities

  d_0 d_(K-R)-d_R d_K >= 0,             0<=R<=K,

it applies every N_Q^2 and checks both the same boundary inequalities
and the complete all-radius current cone of the output.  The zero
failure counts are bounded evidence for square-boundary insertion and
finite square boundary reduction, not proofs of either universal
statement.

Finally it checks whether abstract log-concavity plus every available
no-wall diagonal current forces the off-diagonal no-wall currents.  It
does not: the displayed first diagonal-admissible input [1,2,3,0] has
the exact off-diagonal current -1.

All fusion products and currents use exact integer arithmetic in
boost::multiprecision::cpp_int.

COMPILER FLAGS
g++ -O2 -std=c++20 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Wconversion \
  -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror \
  probe_su2_current_cone_invariance.cpp \
  -o probe_su2_current_cone_invariance

